//
// PROBE: `alias` and `unalias` — binding one name to another at run time.
//
// The **alias is on top** and the target beneath it
// (reference/rust_multistackvm/src/stdlib/alias.rs:9,11), so the source reads
// `<target> <alias> alias`. Neither name has to exist yet: D16's open world
// means a name may be bound before anything answers to it.
//
// This is what makes an alias *cycle* constructible from a program — the
// reference's `register_alias` unregisters and inserts with no check
// (multistackvm_alias.rs:5-15) — and it is why `Registry::follow` resolves to
// a fixed point with a guard rather than a plain loop. The cycle itself is not
// probed here: the reference reports it through a path that carries a
// `~/.cargo` location (F66), so no golden can hold it.
//
"probe: a name aliased to println prints" println
:println :say alias
"aliased" say
"probe: ?alias sees it, and unalias removes it" println
:say ?alias println
:say unalias
:say ?alias println
